course: dictionary
lesson: 10591
title: Big words for bad results
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Underperforming» means doing badly, or not as well as people expected or needed. Adults often use it to sound calm and polite about something that has gone wrong.
explain: «The school was told it was underperforming in maths.» The clue is the inspectors: the school's results were not good enough. Underperforming is a softer way of saying failing, so it is called a euphemism.
explain: In business news, «The company was underperforming» means it was making less money than others. The clue is the money: the context tells you which meaning is needed. To check, swap in «doing badly» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Underperforming means doing badly, or not as well as expected or needed.
fact: It is jargon: a technical word used by people in a certain job, such as managers, teachers and inspectors.
fact: It is a euphemism: a gentle, polite word used instead of a harsher one like «bad» or «failing».
fact: In finance, underperforming means making less money than others in the same field.
fact: The verb forms are underperform, underperforms and underperformed. «The team underperformed last season» means they did worse than expected.
exercise: «The report said the class was underperforming.» What does underperforming mean here? | Being very loud | Winning every race | *Doing worse than expected | Sleeping in class
exercise: «The company was underperforming, so it earned less money than its rivals.» What does underperforming mean in this sentence? | Making more money than others | *Making less money than others | Moving to a new town | Being given a prize
exercise: Pupil says underperforming is just a posh word for bad. Why might an inspector choose it? | It means exactly the same as good | *It sounds calm and polite, and it avoids harsh words | It is shorter than any other word | It is only used in football
test: Which word is the past tense of underperform? | underperforms | *underperformed | underperforming | underperformance
test: What is a euphemism? | *A gentle, polite word used instead of a harsher one | A word with two meanings | A very long word | A word from another country
exam: Which sentence shows that underperforming means doing badly? | *«The team was not playing as well as it should have.» | «The team was swimming in the sea.» | «The team bought new shoes.» | «The team won every match this year.»
